          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $k$ be a field and $n\\geq 1$. The **Heisenberg Lie algebra** $\\mathfrak h_n(k)$ is the $(2n+1)$-dimensional $k$-vector space with basis\n\n$$X_1,\\ldots,X_n,P_1,\\ldots,P_n,Z.$$\n\nIts bracket is the bilinear extension of\n\n$$[P_i,X_j]=\\delta_{ij}Z,\\qquad [X_i,P_j]=-\\delta_{ij}Z,$$\n\nwith all $[X_i,X_j]$, $[P_i,P_j]$, $[Z,X_i]$, and $[Z,P_i]$ equal to zero. Here $\\delta_{ij}=1$ when $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise. Equivalently, for coefficient vectors $a,b,a',b'\\in k^n$,\n\n$$\\left[\\sum_i(a_iX_i+b_iP_i)+cZ,\\sum_i(a_i'X_i+b_i'P_i)+c'Z\\right]=\\left(\\sum_i(b_i a_i'-b_i'a_i)\\right)Z.$$\n\nThe Jacobi identity and alternation, including in characteristic $2$, are proved in [[lem-heisenberg-bracket-defines-lie-algebra]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-lie-algebra-over-a-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $k$ be a field. A **Lie algebra over $k$** is a $k$-vector space\n$\\mathfrak g$ ([[def-vector-space]]) together with a map\n\n$$[-,-]:\\mathfrak g\\times\\mathfrak g\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak g$$\n\nthat is linear in each variable ([[def-linear-map]]) and satisfies, for all\n$x,y,z\\in\\mathfrak g$,\n\n$$[x,x]=0$$\n\nand\n\n$$[x,[y,z]]+[y,[z,x]]+[z,[x,y]]=0.$$\n\nThe first identity is **alternation** and the second is the **Jacobi identity**.\nNo finite-dimensional hypothesis is imposed. Alternation implies\n$[x,y]=-[y,x]$ in every characteristic: expand\n$0=[x+y,x+y]=[x,y]+[y,x]$. Thus skew-symmetry is a consequence here, not a\nreplacement for alternation in characteristic $2$.\n\nThe zero vector space, with its unique bracket, is a Lie algebra. A Lie algebra\nis **abelian** when $[x,y]=0$ for every $x,y$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
